 The energy loss collisions with nuclei (\(S\)) is described by the equation \(S_{Z W} = 4 Z^{2} A^{-1} E^{-1} \left[\frac{Z^{2}}{137 A} + \ln \left(\frac{E}{I} \right) \right]\). The energy loss collisions with electrons (\(S_{e}\)) is described by the equation \(S_{e W} = 0.704 Z \left(\frac{1}{A} \right)^{1 / 3} \ln \left(\frac{2 m_{e} c^{2} E }{I^{2}} \right)\) . We can find the energy losses by dividing both equations by $E$, so we are left with a linear proportionality. The ratio is then, $$\frac{S_{\text {nucleus } } }{S_{e} }
=\frac{4 Z^{2} A^{-2} \left[\frac{Z^{2}}{137 A} + \ln \left(\frac{E}{I} \right) \right]}
{0.704 Z A^{-1 / 3} \ln \left(\frac{2 m_{e} c^{2} E }{I^{2}} \right)} .$$. Let's simplify this ratio. $$\frac{S_{\text {nuclei }} }{S_{e} }
=\frac{4 Z A^{-5 / 3} \left[\frac{Z^{2}}{137 A} + \ln \left(\frac{E}{I} \right) \right]}
{0.704 \ln \left(\frac{2 m_{e} c^{2} E}{I^{2}} \right)} .$$ Now we know that the alpha particle has Z =
2, the energy is 4 \(MeV\), mass is $m_{\alpha} = 6.64466 \times 10^{-27}\)kg. We also know the atomic mass of carbon \(A = 12\). We do not know the value \(I\) ("mean excitation energy"). However, it will cancel out in the equation, allowing us to simplify it further.  $$\frac{S_{\text {nuclei }} }{S_{e} }
=\frac{4 \cdot 2 \cdot 12^{-5 / 3} \left[\frac{2^{2}}{137 \cdot 12} + \ln \left(\frac{4}{I} \right) \right]}
{0.704 \ln \left(\frac{2 \cdot (9.11 \times 10^{-31} \mathrm{kg}) \cdot
(3 \times 10^8)^{2} \cdot 4 \cdot 1.6 \times 10^{-13} }{I^{2}} \right)} .$$. We only have to take our calculator to find the value of the ratio. Then the answer is (E).
